<div class="wrap">
  <h1>Drinking, a recent stroke, and Alzheimer’s</h1>
  <p class="lede">Two different people. Alcohol makes both situations more dangerous, for different reasons. This is a plain explanation of those risks. It is not a diagnosis and it does not replace the doctors already treating them.</p>

  <div class="pair">
    <div class="who">
      <span>Patient 1</span>
      <strong>Just had a stroke, still drinks, and is on a blood thinner</strong>
      <p>The urgent risk is another stroke, including a bleed in the brain. Alcohol and a blood thinner together raise that chance. She should not drink while this is being sorted out, and she should not stop the blood thinner on her own.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="who">
      <span>Patient 2</span>
      <strong>Alzheimer’s, and getting noticeably worse</strong>
      <p>Alzheimer’s usually slips over months and years. A clear drop over days or weeks is often something extra on top of it. Alcohol can be that extra layer, and so can illness, a new stroke, or a medication problem.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="urgent">
    <strong>Call emergency services now</strong> for either person if there is sudden weakness or numbness, a drooping face, trouble speaking, a sudden severe headache, repeated vomiting, a new fall with a hit to the head, black or bloody stools, vomiting blood, a seizure, or confusion that is sharply worse than their usual. On a blood thinner, a head injury is an emergency even if they seem okay at first.
  </div>

  <h2>What “a drink” means</h2>
  <p>One standard drink is about 12 ounces of regular beer, 5 ounces of wine, or 1.5 ounces of liquor. A large pour of wine is often two drinks. “A few” in the evening can already be a heavy day.</p>
  <p>Health agencies describe heavy drinking as more than 7 drinks a week for a woman, or more than 14 for a man, and binge drinking as 4 or more drinks in about two hours for a woman, or 5 or more for a man. Those ceilings are for otherwise healthy adults. They are not a safe allowance after a stroke, on a blood thinner, or with dementia.</p>

  <h2>How alcohol leads to stroke</h2>
  <p>A stroke is brain tissue dying because blood flow fails. In an ischemic stroke a clot blocks an artery. In a hemorrhagic stroke a vessel tears and blood floods the brain. Alcohol pushes both.</p>
  <h3>Blood pressure</h3>
  <p>Alcohol raises blood pressure, during a drinking session and over months of regular drinking. High blood pressure is the strongest thing a person can change that causes stroke. A spike can burst a small deep artery, or it can crack plaque in a neck artery so a clot forms and travels into the brain.</p>
  <h3>The heart rhythm</h3>
  <p>A heavy bout of drinking can set off atrial fibrillation, sometimes in a person with no known heart disease. In that rhythm the upper chamber of the heart quivers, blood sits still, and a clot can form and shoot to the brain. That is a common cause of a large ischemic stroke. Repeated drinking also weakens heart muscle over years, which is another source of clots.</p>
  <h3>The morning after</h3>
  <p>After a binge the body is often dehydrated, blood pressure rebounds, and platelets become stickier. That window, not only the hours of drinking, is when ischemic strokes show up. People describe going to bed “fine” and waking with a weak arm or slurred speech.</p>
  <h3>Bleeding</h3>
  <p>Alcohol makes platelets work worse, irritates the stomach, and, when drinking has been heavy for years, damages the liver that makes clotting proteins. That shifts the risk toward a bleed: in the stomach, and in the brain. Heavy drinking is tied to hemorrhagic stroke in large studies, including INTERSTROKE. The same person can be at risk of both a clot and a bleed. Alcohol does not pick one lane.</p>

  <h2>Patient 1: the recent stroke, the drinking, and the blood thinner</h2>
  <p>She already had a stroke. The brain injury is fresh. The job of the blood thinner is to stop the next clot. The job of the next weeks is also to stop a bleed into that injured brain. Drinking works against both.</p>

  <div class="stop">
    <strong>Do not stop the blood thinner because of this page.</strong> After a stroke, stopping it on her own can let a clot form within days. The prescribing doctor is the one who changes or holds it. What she can do today is stop the alcohol.
  </div>

  <h3>Why the combination is worse than either one alone</h3>
  <p>Blood thinners come in two families. Anticoagulants include warfarin and the newer drugs apixaban (Eliquis), rivaroxaban (Xarelto), and dabigatran (Pradaxa). Antiplatelet drugs include aspirin and clopidogrel (Plavix). All of them make bleeding more likely. Alcohol adds four extra problems:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Falls.</strong> Alcohol slows balance and judgment. A fall on a blood thinner can tear a vein under the skull. The blood collects slowly. The person may seem only a little drowsy, then worsen over hours. That is a subdural hematoma, and it is an emergency.</li>
    <li><strong>Bleeding into the stroke itself.</strong> In the first days and weeks, injured brain can bleed even without a fall. Doctors already accept some of that risk to prevent another clot. Alcohol increases it through blood pressure, weaker clotting, and another injury.</li>
    <li><strong>Unpredictable strength of the thinner.</strong> With warfarin, a night of drinking can push the INR too high, which means the blood is thinner than the clinic intended. Long-term heavy drinking and liver disease swing the INR the other way too, so the dose that was safe last month is not safe this month. The newer anticoagulants are not watched with an INR, but a sick liver, a drinking binge, and a fall still raise the chance of a major bleed. Aspirin plus alcohol is a classic cause of a stomach bleed: black stool, vomiting blood, or feeling faint.</li>
    <li><strong>Another ischemic stroke.</strong> The thinner does not cancel the blood-pressure spike, the atrial fibrillation, or the sticky-platelet rebound from drinking. People on blood thinners still have strokes. Alcohol is one of the reasons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The American Heart Association’s guidance for someone who has already had a stroke or a TIA is to avoid heavy drinking, and if they drink at all to stay at or under 1 drink a day for a woman and 2 for a man. That is a ceiling for stable, long-term prevention. It is not permission in the weeks after a stroke, and it is not permission while the blood thinner dose is still being settled. For her, the safe amount right now is none, until the stroke doctor says otherwise.</p>
  <p>If she has been drinking most days, stopping suddenly can cause shakes, sweating, confusion, or a seizure, usually in the first one to three days. That is a medical problem, not a character problem. Tell the doctor how much she actually drinks so they can stop it safely. Hiding the amount leads to the wrong thinner dose.</p>

  <h2>How alcohol relates to Alzheimer’s</h2>
  <p>Alzheimer’s is a disease that slowly destroys memory circuits, especially the hippocampus, and then language, judgment, and the ability to manage a day. Alcohol injures the same regions. Heavy drinking over years shrinks the brain, including the hippocampus, and it damages the small vessels that feed it. The 2020 and 2024 Lancet Commission reports on dementia list heavy alcohol use as one of the risks a person can still change.</p>
  <p>Three things get mixed together and should not be:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Alzheimer’s disease itself</strong>, which does not reverse when drinking stops.</li>
    <li><strong>Alcohol on top of Alzheimer’s</strong>, which adds confusion, poor sleep, falls, and bad reactions to medicines. That layer can improve when the drinking stops. Families sometimes see a person seem clearer over a few weeks. That is the alcohol fog lifting, not the Alzheimer’s going away.</li>
    <li><strong>Alcohol-related brain injury</strong>, including thiamine deficiency. Heavy drinking uses up vitamin B1. Too little of it causes Wernicke’s encephalopathy: confusion, unsteady walking, and eye-movement trouble. Left untreated it becomes Korsakoff’s syndrome, a severe memory loss that looks like dementia and is only partly reversible. This is a reason to tell a doctor about drinking instead of assuming “the Alzheimer’s is just worse.”</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Alcohol also feeds the stroke problem into the dementia problem. High blood pressure, atrial fibrillation, and small-vessel damage cause vascular brain injury. Many people with Alzheimer’s have both. Each small stroke makes the Alzheimer’s look like it jumped downhill.</p>

  <h2>Patient 2: Alzheimer’s that is getting noticeably worse</h2>
  <p>True Alzheimer’s progression is usually gradual. A change that family can point to — more lost, more sleepy, up all night, not walking as well, a new anger, not recognizing people they knew last month — needs a cause looked for. Alcohol is one cause. It is not the only one, and the others matter just as much.</p>
  <h3>If this person drinks</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Even one or two drinks can sedate someone with Alzheimer’s much more than it used to. The same pour that was “fine” at 60 can cause a fall and a night of confusion at 75.</li>
    <li>Alcohol breaks sleep into pieces. The person is up, agitated, and then drowsy and more confused the next day. That cycle gets called “sundowning” and gets treated with more pills, which then interact with the next drink.</li>
    <li>Memory medicines such as donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine, and memantine do not mix well with alcohol. Alcohol adds dizziness and sedation. Sleeping pills, anxiety pills, bladder pills, and antipsychotic pills plus alcohol are a common path to a fall and a hospital delirium.</li>
    <li>They may forget they already drank and pour again. Count what is actually disappearing from the bottles, not what they report.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>A fast drop is not “just Alzheimer’s”</h3>
  <p>Ask the doctor to look for things that make dementia suddenly worse and that can be treated: a urine infection, dehydration, constipation, pain, low oxygen, a new medicine, depression, poor hearing, and an unwitnessed stroke or a series of small ones. If the worse days line up with drinking, say so. If they drink heavily, ask specifically about thiamine and about withdrawal. A sudden change still deserves a same-week appointment, and the emergency signs above still apply. A new one-sided weakness or a new speech problem in a person with Alzheimer’s is a stroke until a doctor shows that it is not.</p>

  <h2>What helps now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Patient 1: no alcohol. Keep every dose of the blood thinner exactly as prescribed. Bring the real drinking history to the next visit, including binge nights. Ask whether she needs a blood-pressure check, a heart-rhythm check, and, if she is on warfarin, a sooner INR.</li>
    <li>Patient 2: remove alcohol from the house if they cannot reliably limit it. Write down when the worsening started, what changed, and which medicines they take. Ask the clinician to separate “Alzheimer’s progression” from infection, medication, stroke, and alcohol.</li>
    <li>Neither person should be shamed into hiding it. Hidden drinking is how the thinner dose and the dementia plan both go wrong.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="src">General sources behind this explanation include the American Heart Association and American Stroke Association secondary-prevention guidance, the INTERSTROKE study, the National Institute on Alcohol Abuse and Alcoholism definitions of heavy and binge drinking, and the Lancet Commission on dementia prevention (2020 and 2024). Doses, the type of blood thinner, and whether any alcohol is ever acceptable again are decisions for the clinicians who know these two people.</p>
